  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 135 PM CDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nPrecipitation/Storm Trends This Week...\n\nFor this afternoon and evening...dry conditions will continue \nearly this afternoon as the cap is holding. Ingredients still \ncoming together for a conditional severe weather threat later this\nafternoon into this evening, peak threat time between 4-10pm. \nInstability is building with SBCAPEs between 2000-3500 J/kg over \nmuch of the area, but clouds have limited insolation in some \nspots. As a weak mid-level shortwave and surface frontal boundary \ntraverse the area this afternoon, the combination of the eroding \ncap, building instability, deep-layer shear of 35-45 kts, and steep\nmid-level lapse rates should allow the cap to erode enough for \nisolated to scattered shower/storm development. The prime spot for\ndevelopment looks to be the corridor between Rhinelander and \nShawano between 4-6pm, where the cap will be the weakest and the \nforcing will be the greatest. Some lake/bay boundary interaction \ncould also help initiate/focus convection near the MI border. The \nclouds could also create a differential temp gradient, which could\nserve as a initiation spot for storms. The shower/storm activity \nwill shift east/southeast through the early evening, likely ending\nby 10-11pm as capping strengthens and instability weakens. The \nmost intense storms will be capable of producing damaging winds \nand hail. Impressive PWATs of 1.7-1.9\" will support locally heavy \nrainfall in any storm as well.\n\nOvernight into early Wednesday...as the frontal boundary\nsags/hovers to our south, mainly dry conditions are expected \ninto Tuesday afternoon. The next period of concern will be late \nTuesday night into early Wednesday, as another shortwave/MCV is \nforecast to ride along the boundary, likely setting off an area of\nconvection which will race east, arriving in the early morning\nhours of Wednesday. Still some uncertainty on the exact track of \nthis feature, but many CAMs are showing it impacting at least the\nsouthern half of the CWA. HRRR Neural Network really starting to \nhighlight southern MN and northern IA Tuesday evening then \nspreading the highlighted threat region into parts of WI. SPC \nkept the Marginal Risk hugging the southern CWA, but this area may\nneed to be expanded.\n\nLate This Week and Weekend...the frontal boundary will linger in\nthe western Great Lakes for most of the period, with a pool of\nmoisture/unstable air either just to our southwest or in the area.\nThis will create intermittent shower and storm chances. Severe \nclusters of storms, possibly MCSs, will be possible as we remain \non the northern edge of the heat dome over the central Plains. \n\nTemperatures/Humidity...heat index readings will top out in the\n90s for much of the area this afternoon. Despite the cold front \nmoving through this evening, temps on Tuesday will be close or\njust slightly cooler than today. It will also still be humid on \nTuesday, as dew points remain in the 60s to low 70s across east-\ncentral WI, but locations in north-central WI will see a little \nrelief with dew points forecast in the 50s to low 60s.",
